doc0:Gumball (video game) Gumball is a 1983 video game written for the Apple II by by Robert Cook (now Veda Hlubinka-Cook) and published by Broderbund. It was ported to the Atari 8-bit family, and Commodore 64. The player controls the valves of a maze-like machine to sort gumballs by their color. Broderbund co-founder Doug Carlston conceived of the game's machine-sorting concept, and Cook added the gumball conceit and built the core gameplay within a week. The game contains a secret image that was only found three decades after the game's release. Reviewers separately noted the difficulty of the game's later
