doc1:Oryzomyini Oryzomyini, or rice rat, is a tribe of rodents in the subfamily Sigmodontinae of family Cricetidae. It includes about 120 species in about thirty genera, distributed from the eastern United States to the southernmost parts of South America, including many offshore islands. It is part of the clade Oryzomyalia, which includes most of the South American Sigmodontinae. The name ""Oryzomyini"" derives from that of its type genus, ""Oryzomys"", which means ""rice rat"" or ""rice mouse"". Many species are also known as rice rats. An oryzomyine group was first envisaged by Oldfield Thomas in the early 20th century. He defined
